<BaseLayout pageId="lawrence-sewer-lateral-repair">
  <div class="page-intro">
    <div class="container page-intro__inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">In-depth local guide · Lawrence, Indiana</p>
      <h1>Sewer lateral repair in Lawrence, Indiana</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        In Lawrence, the sewer lateral that runs from your house to the public main is yours — including
        the part under the street right-of-way — and repairing it requires a permit from the City. If the
        repair is done by lining or pipe bursting, the City's current policy also requires camera video of
        the finished work before it is approved.
      </p>

      <dl class="status-panel status-panel--four">
        <div class="status-panel__pending">
          <dt>Technical review</dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>Pending.</strong> Not yet reviewed by a qualified sewer professional, and the open
            questions below have not been confirmed with Lawrence Utilities.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Search engines</dt>
          <dd><strong>Not submitted.</strong> Publicly readable, but not approved for search indexing.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Sources</dt>
          <dd>
            <strong>8 cited documents</strong>, 7 of them published by the City of Lawrence or its
            utility. <a href="#sources">Each lists the date it was verified.</a>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Independence</dt>
          <dd>Not affiliated with the City or Lawrence Utilities. No contractor sponsors or edits this guide.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="container article-layout">
    <OnThisPage items={toc} />

    <article class="article-body">
      <section class="summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
        <h2 class="summary__title" id="summary-title">The short version</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <EvidenceChip kind="fact" />
            <span><strong>The whole lateral is the owner's.</strong> The ordinance's "building sewer" runs to the public sewer and includes the portion in the right-of-way.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <EvidenceChip kind="fact" />
            <span><strong>A repair needs a City permit.</strong> Both the ordinance and the utility's policy manual say so.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <EvidenceChip kind="fact" />
            <span><strong>Lining and pipe bursting are allowed</strong> under the utility policy the City currently links, alongside excavation.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <EvidenceChip kind="fact" />
            <span><strong>Trenchless repairs are approved on video.</strong> Camera footage of the finished work goes to the utility.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <EvidenceChip kind="unverified" />
            <span><strong>Still open:</strong> responsibility for the tap or wye at the main, the permit fee, and where the application is filed today.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <EvidenceNote kind="fact" label="How to read this guide">
        <p>
          Every Lawrence-specific statement below is tied to a cited City ordinance, utility document, or
          other recorded Lawrence source, listed under <a href="#sources">sources</a>. Descriptions of how
          repair methods physically work are cited to engineering specifications issued elsewhere; they
          explain the methods and are <strong>not</strong> Lawrence requirements.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reasoning drawn from several sources is labeled <strong>Inference</strong>, and questions the
          public record does not answer are labeled <strong>Not established</strong>. Neither is ever
          presented as a verified rule.
        </p>
      </EvidenceNote>

      <h2 id="what-is-yours">What counts as your pipe</h2>
      <p>
        Lawrence ordinance calls it the <strong>building sewer</strong>: the pipe that runs from your
        building drain to the public sewer. The definition expressly includes the portion of that pipe
        inside the public right-of-way. The building drain itself is treated as ending five feet outside
        the building wall, so almost everything between your foundation and the public main is the
        building sewer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Other words for the same pipe are <em>lateral</em>, <em>private lateral</em>, and{' '}
        <em>sewer service line</em>. The utility's newsletters use "sanitary sewer lateral" for it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="responsibility">Who is responsible for repairs</h2>
      <p>
        The ordinance places responsibility on the property owner who benefits from the connection:
        providing, installing, and connecting it, and making all necessary repairs, extensions,
        relocations, changes, or replacements. The utility has said the same thing in plain language to
        homeowners twice — in 2023, and again in August 2024, when it stated that residents are
        responsible for the maintenance and repair of their sanitary sewer laterals to the point of
        connection at the city's main sewer line.
      </p>
      <p>
        The ordinance also lets the Director alter, modify, or waive those requirements where compliance
        is not possible because of extenuating circumstances.
      </p>

      <EvidenceNote kind="inference">
        <p>
          The 2019 policy manual describes owner responsibility "from the property line to the point of
          connection with the public main." Read alone, that sentence could be mistaken for a rule that
          the City owns the pipe up to the property line. Read together with the ordinance definition
          (which includes the right-of-way portion) and the 2023 and 2024 newsletters (structure to main),
          the sources point the same way: the owner is responsible for the full lateral. This
          reconciliation is our reasoning from consistent sources, not a single statement in one of them.
        </p>
      </EvidenceNote>

      <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
        <p>
          We have not established who is responsible for the <strong>tap or wye at the main itself</strong>,
          how often the Director's waiver is used in practice, or whether any cost-share or assistance
          program exists. We are also not publishing a property-line responsibility diagram until the
          tap question is confirmed with Lawrence Utilities, because a diagram would imply an answer we
          do not have.
        </p>
      </EvidenceNote>

      <h2 id="before-you-hire">Before you hire anyone: find out whose pipe is failing</h2>
      <p>
        Lawrence Utilities asks residents with a sanitary sewer problem to call its after-hours on-call
        line first, and gives a direct financial reason: the problem may be in the public main, and if you
        hire a contractor who comes out and determines the fault is in the public main, the utility does
        not reimburse those contractor costs. In its August 2024 newsletter the utility published{' '}
        <strong>317-260-0220</strong> as that after-hours on-call number.
      </p>
      <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
        <p>
          That number was published in August 2024 and this project has not re-verified it since. Check
          the current contact details on the City of Lawrence Utilities website before you rely on it.
        </p>
      </EvidenceNote>
      <p>
        The same newsletter notes something worth knowing if work has recently been done in your street:
        the utility reported an increase in sewer laterals damaged by contractors installing underground
        fiber optic lines for 5G upgrades, and said it can help where damage is determined to result from
        a third-party contractor working in the area. The utility does not say what that help consists of,
        and neither will we.
      </p>

      <h2 id="diagnosis">What a camera inspection can and cannot settle</h2>
      <p>
        A camera inspection is what turns "the drain keeps backing up" into something you can act on. A
        CCTV run through the line helps identify the type and condition of a defect, where it sits along
        the length of the line, and how much of the line is affected.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it does not by itself establish is where that point is on the surface, or how deep the pipe
        is buried. Locating the line and its depth generally takes separate locating equipment or further
        investigation by the contractor. And which repair method suits your line is a professional
        judgment that depends on the pipe, the defect, the route, and site conditions together — this page
        does not make it for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lawrence's policy manual treats camera work as a normal part of lateral work in both directions:
        the City may require a lateral camera inspection, with the video provided, instead of excavating
        to expose the pipe, and it requires video of the finished repair for trenchless methods (below).
      </p>

      <DecisionFlow />

      <p>In sequence, that is:</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>A symptom appears — repeated backups, slow drains, or a finding from a camera.</li>
        <li>Establish whether the failure is in your lateral or in the public main.</li>
        <li>If it is the main, it is the utility's to fix. If it is your lateral, the decision is yours.</li>
        <li>A camera inspection identifies the defect and how much of the line is affected.</li>
        <li>You and the contractor choose a method: spot repair, full-length lining, pipe bursting, or open-cut replacement.</li>
        <li>The work is permitted, inspected, and — for lining or bursting — verified on video before it is accepted.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="methods">The repair methods you are choosing between</h2>
      <p>
        Lawrence's policy manual states plainly that the utility <strong>does allow</strong> rehabilitation
        of laterals using pipe-bursting and lining technologies with appropriate materials. That is the
        Lawrence-specific fact that matters here: under the City's current published policy both
        trenchless approaches are available alongside conventional excavation.
      </p>
      <p class="subtle">
        How each method physically works, and what the work involves. Which one suits your line is
        decided by the inspection and the contractor, not by this page.
      </p>

      <ul class="methods" aria-label="Repair method comparison">
        <li>
          <MethodSchematic kind="spot" />
          <h3>Spot or sectional repair</h3>
          <dl>
            <dt>What happens</dt>
            <dd>A short section is repaired, or a sectional liner is set across one defect.</dd>
            <dt>What the work involves</dt>
            <dd>
              Lawrence's policy contemplates this: for a point repair with a sectional liner, the
              post-repair video must run from the cleanout to at least two feet past the repaired
              section.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </li>
        <li>
          <MethodSchematic kind="cipp" />
          <h3>Full-length lining (CIPP)</h3>
          <dl>
            <dt>What happens</dt>
            <dd>
              A flexible tube impregnated with a thermoset resin is installed inside the existing pipe
              and cured in place, forming a hard, impermeable liner pipe inside the host pipe.
            </dd>
            <dt>What the work involves</dt>
            <dd>
              The line is cleaned and inspected first; an obstruction that cannot be cleared by
              conventional cleaning or cutting equipment has to be dealt with before lining. Sewer
              service is interrupted while the liner is installed and cured.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </li>
        <li>
          <MethodSchematic kind="bursting" />
          <h3>Pipe bursting</h3>
          <dl>
            <dt>What happens</dt>
            <dd>
              The old pipe is fractured in place and its fragments pushed outward into the soil while a
              new HDPE or PVC pipe is pulled into the space left behind.
            </dd>
            <dt>What the work involves</dt>
            <dd>
              Excavation is still needed for an insertion pit and a reception pit, and specifications
              set clearances from nearby structures and buried utilities.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </li>
        <li>
          <MethodSchematic kind="open-cut" />
          <h3>Open-cut replacement</h3>
          <dl>
            <dt>What happens</dt>
            <dd>The pipe is excavated and replaced.</dd>
            <dt>What the work involves</dt>
            <dd>
              Lawrence's installation standards then apply in full — materials, size, depth, slope,
              backfill, cleanouts, tracer wire, and inspection before backfilling. Surface restoration
              becomes part of the job.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="methods-note">
        The drawings are simplified schematics of each general method. They are not to scale and show
        no Lawrence-specific pipe, depth, or site.
      </p>

      <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
        <p>
          We publish no lifespan, price, success rate, or "best method" claim, because we have no evidence
          for any of them. How long a repair lasts and what it costs in Lawrence are questions for a
          qualified contractor and, for this page, for the expert review it has not yet had.
        </p>
      </EvidenceNote>

      <h2 id="city-work">What the City's own sewer work tells you</h2>
      <p>
        Lawrence publishes what it is doing to its own sanitary system, and two things in that record are
        useful to a homeowner.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, the utility has publicly identified <strong>older clay sewer laterals</strong> as a local
        problem: it asks owners to replace clay laterals that are beyond their useful life, and says old
        clay pipe cracks and breaks easily, letting dirt and roots in and causing backups. It does not say
        how common clay laterals are in Lawrence, and neither do we.
      </p>
      <p>
        Second, the City is actively rehabilitating parts of its system with the same family of methods
        being discussed for private laterals, including lateral lining inside its own projects.
      </p>

      <p class="projects__caption" id="projects-caption">
        Recent Lawrence sanitary sewer rehabilitation, compiled from the City's own documents. These
        are <strong>public-system</strong> projects.
      </p>
      <ul class="projects" aria-labelledby="projects-caption">
        {
          rehabProjects.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <h3>{p.project}</h3>
              <dl>
                <div>
                  <dt>Area</dt>
                  <dd>{p.area}</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <dt>Documented method</dt>
                  <dd>{p.method}</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <dt>Documented quantity</dt>
                  <dd>{p.quantity}</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <dt>Source</dt>
                  <dd>
                    <a href={p.href}>{p.source}</a>
                  </dd>
                </div>
              </dl>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <EvidenceNote kind="unverified" label="Important limit">
        <p>
          These are the City's projects on the <strong>public</strong> system. That Lawrence lines its own
          sewers, and lines laterals inside its own contracts, does <strong>not</strong> establish that
          your lateral can be lined, that lining is the right choice for it, or what it would cost. Only
          an inspection of your line and a qualified contractor can answer that.
        </p>
      </EvidenceNote>

      <p>
        One practical detail from the City's 2026 lining contract is worth carrying into your own
        conversation with a contractor, as a question rather than a rule: on that project the contractor
        must give affected customers 24 to 48 hours' written notice, because building sewer laterals are
        out of service during installation, curing, and restoration, with a maximum of eight hours without
        service. Your private repair is not governed by that contract, but the same physical constraint
        applies — so ask how long your line will be out of service and what you should avoid during it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="permit">The permit Lawrence requires</h2>
      <p>
        Two separate sources say a permit is required. The ordinance makes it unlawful to repair, modify,
        or connect a building sewer without a valid sanitary sewer connection permit issued by the
        Director. The utility's policy manual requires a permit before repair, replacement, or relocation
        of any lateral connected to the City's sanitary sewer.
      </p>

      <h3 id="permit-sequence">From application to approval, as documented</h3>
      <ol class="process">
        <li>
          <strong>Apply before the work</strong>{' '}
          The ordinance and the policy manual both require the permit first. For emergency repair work,
          the current form says to apply no later than the following business day.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Start within 150 days</strong>{' '}
          The permit expires if work has not begun within 150 days of issuance, subject to an extension
          for good cause.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Inspection, or video for trenchless work</strong>{' '}
          Open-cut work needs at least 24 hours' notice and an exposed line for inspection (the Director
          may waive it). For lining and bursting, camera video of the finished repair is submitted to the
          utility for approval.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Approval</strong>{' '}
          The utility may withhold approval of a lined or burst repair if the video shows a defective
          repair.
        </li>
        <li class="process__open">
          <strong>Not established</strong>{' '}
          The sewer-lateral permit fee, where the application is filed today, and the video format the
          utility accepts now.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3 id="permit-form">What the current form asks for</h3>
      <p>
        The City publishes the current form: the{' '}
        <a
          href="https://www.cityoflawrence.org/sites/default/files/document-group/Sanitary%20Sewer%20Permit%20Application.pdf"
          rel="nofollow noopener">Application for Sanitary Building Sewer Lateral Construction Permit</a
        >. What it actually asks for:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          the type of work — it has boxes for <strong>New Line</strong>,{' '}
          <strong>Repair/Modification Work</strong>, and <strong>Replace/Relocate</strong>, so a repair is
          squarely within it;
        </li>
        <li>
          whether the work is an emergency. For emergency repair work, the form states the application must
          be made <strong>no later than the following business day</strong>;
        </li>
        <li>
          a plat drawing of the proposed project showing the location of work and the materials being used,
          carrying the statement that all work will meet or exceed City of Lawrence standards and
          specifications;
        </li>
        <li>the service address, property owner details, assessor parcel number, and legal description;</li>
        <li>
          the installing or repairing contractor's details, including a <strong>Lawrence registration
          number</strong> and its expiration date;
        </li>
        <li>a note that a separate grease trap application is required where a project needs one.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The ordinance separately requires owner and contractor information, the premises, and plans showing
        materials of construction and the installation method; the policy manual adds a drawing of existing
        and proposed structures and utilities. The obligation sits with the owner.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Who applies:</strong> Under ordinance § 5-1-2-1(E), an application normally comes from a
        plumbing contractor licensed by Indiana, Marion County, and Lawrence, or from another contractor
        meeting the City Building Commissioner's bond and insurance requirements. The Director may
        accept other applicants at the Director's discretion. Before work starts, ask the contractor
        who will apply, which registrations apply, and who will coordinate the City inspection. The
        ordinance also says the permit expires if work has not begun within 150 days of issuance,
        subject to an extension for good cause (§ 5-1-2-1(G)).
      </p>
      <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
        <p>
          The form <strong>does not list a sewer-lateral permit fee or a submission address</strong>, and
          it carries no revision date. Its statement that there is no fee applies only to the{' '}
          <strong>separate grease-trap application</strong>, not the sewer-lateral permit. The 2019
          policy manual says application fees are due but lists a DPW office address we have not
          confirmed is current. Confirm the actual sewer-lateral fee, the submission method, and the
          current office with Lawrence Utilities before you plan around them.
        </p>
      </EvidenceNote>

      <h2 id="inspection">Inspection, and the video that gets trenchless work approved</h2>
      <p>
        For work done in the open, the ordinance requires at least 24 hours' notice that the sewer work is
        ready for inspection, and the building sewer must be exposed from the foundation to the connection
        so it can be inspected. The Director may waive that inspection, with or without conditions, and has
        a right of entry to inspect.
      </p>
      <p>For lining and pipe bursting, the policy manual sets a different kind of proof:</p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          the contractor submits camera video of the repair to the utility for the work to be approved;
        </li>
        <li>
          for a full rehabilitation, the video has to cover the whole lateral, from the cleanout near the
          structure to the connection at the public main;
        </li>
        <li>
          for a point repair with a sectional liner, the video has to run from the cleanout to at least two
          feet past the repaired section;
        </li>
        <li>the utility may withhold approval if the video shows a defective repair.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A practical question to ask before the work starts: are producing and submitting that post-repair
        video included in the quoted scope, and who is responsible for them?
      </p>
      <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
        <p>
          The 2019 policy specifies DVD. This project has not verified the format Lawrence currently
          accepts.
        </p>
      </EvidenceNote>

      <h2 id="replacement">If the line is replaced rather than rehabilitated</h2>
      <p>The policy manual's installation requirements are specific. Among them:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the owner hires a contractor licensed, bonded, and insured in the City of Lawrence;</li>
        <li>approved materials — SDR35 gasketed pipe, Schedule 40 PVC, or Schedule 80 PVC;</li>
        <li>4-inch or 6-inch pipe, with a minimum 6-inch pipe inside the City right-of-way;</li>
        <li>
          at least 18 inches of cover at the building, and 4 feet beyond ten feet from the building where
          the stub depth allows, with minimum slopes set by pipe size;
        </li>
        <li>
          granular backfill to City standards where the lateral runs in or under a driveway or sidewalk, or
          within five feet of a roadway;
        </li>
        <li>cleanouts 1.5 to 3 feet from the building, at bends, and at least every 100 feet;</li>
        <li>tracer tape or wire;</li>
        <li>no backfilling before the utility's inspection, with 24 hours' notice for that inspection.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        One more thing the utility asks of owners, which is cheap to check: sump pumps and downspouts must
        not be connected to the sanitary lateral. The utility cites city ordinance section 5-1-2-2 and says
        those discharges have to go to storm sewer pipes or ditches. It also asks owners to make sure the
        cleanout cap is sealed and secure.
      </p>
      <EvidenceNote kind="unverified">
        <p>
          The installation rules are written for new or replacement installation. How far each one applies
          to a trenchless rehabilitation — the pipe material pulled through a burst, for instance — was not
          confirmed with the utility. We also did not re-read ordinance section 5-1-2-2 itself; that
          citation is the utility's.
        </p>
      </EvidenceNote>

      <h2 id="open-questions">What this page does not tell you yet</h2>
      <p>
        Being explicit about the gaps is part of the method. As of the last verification date, this project
        has <strong>not</strong> established:
      </p>
      <div class="open-questions">
        <ul>
          <li>the current permit fee, or which office accepts the application today;</li>
          <li>the video format the utility accepts today;</li>
          <li>who is responsible for the tap or wye at the main itself;</li>
          <li>whether every address inside Lawrence city limits is served by Lawrence Utilities sanitary sewer;</li>
          <li>how often the Director's waiver is granted, and on what basis;</li>
          <li>whether any cost-share or assistance program exists for homeowners;</li>
          <li>whether the City applies its 2019 policy today without unpublished changes;</li>
          <li>how common clay laterals are in Lawrence housing stock, or how those lines typically fail here;</li>
          <li>what lateral repair costs in Lawrence.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each of those is a question for Lawrence Utilities or your contractor. When we verify one, it will
        appear here with its source, or it will stay off the page.
      </p>

